        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best heavy duty treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home buyers, the Spirit XT485: a 22 by 60-inch deck, a 4.0 HP motor and a lifetime frame, motor and deck warranty on Spirit's US residential terms, on a folding frame, at about $2,649 on Amazon on 6 November 2026. The Horizon Fitness 7.4 AT gives a 22-inch deck and a continuous-duty motor for about $1,588.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What makes a treadmill heavy duty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A stiff, heavy steel frame, a motor rated in continuous horsepower rather than peak, a wide and long deck — 22 by 60 inches is the commercial standard — and a long warranty on the frame and motor. A high weight rating is a useful summary, but only when those other features back it up.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is there a treadmill for 500 lb?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes, but few. On Amazon, the ECHANFIT Heavy Duty Treadmill claims a 500 lb capacity for about $800, though its deck is 50 inches long and its listing states no warranty. Machines from established brands that are rated that high are mostly commercial models sold through dealers, at several times the price.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much weight can a heavy duty treadmill hold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Ratings on established-brand home treadmills commonly run from 300 to about 400 lb, and some go higher. Budget machines sometimes claim 400 to 600 lb. Choose a rating comfortably above your weight, check it on the exact listing, and favour ratings backed by a wide deck, a continuous-duty motor and a long frame warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a folding treadmill heavy duty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be. The Spirit XT485 and NordicTrack Commercial 2450 both fold and have 22-inch decks and long motor warranties. A non-folding frame is generally stiffer, because the hinge is a point of movement, so for very heavy daily use a non-folding machine is the safer choice.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do I need a mat under a heavy duty treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is a good idea. A firm, dense rubber mat larger than the treadmill's footprint protects the floor from the feet, catches belt dust and lubricant and reduces some vibration. Avoid soft foam under a heavy machine, which can let the frame rock.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

